From c8aeb9ecdc77246a1cbf754742ffcfba894b91e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iitimii <timiiowolabi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 09:45:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 30/32] S3.3: cap opener budget, handle conf in extension,
 verify-seed gating

Fix round on the C3 review's five Important findings.

I1: --hard-mode verify had silently become an overlap site. The opener span
becomes run_verify_turn's seed, and the seed is only INGESTED as context --
never judged against the expert's distribution -- so extending it multiplied
unverified front text inside the one mode whose promise is that the expert
approved what you are reading. The extension loop is now gated on
hard_mode != "verify"; verify keeps C2's single deterministic opener.

I2: the "routed tier owns the majority" invariant was still breakable through
the UNCAPPED opener (oopts.n_predict = p.n_predict): at -n 64 the opener alone
could consume the whole answer, no extension needed. The cap is now computed
once as front_cap = min(3*seg_max, n_predict/2) and applied to the opener AND
the extension together, so it bounds everything the front writes in a turn.

I3: the extension armed the confidence monitor with no handler -- a fired
monitor truncated the segment and changed nothing. cut=="conf" now breaks the
overlap and falls through to the blocking acquire, which is the right
escalation; the append-only-front caveat (no checkpoint, so never conf-cut) is
documented where it bites rather than left implicit.

I4: the mlock comment claimed a bundle member locks "its own ~100 MiB". It
locks from the MAPPING BASE, so the range is header-inclusive: 121.5 MiB for
the trio front, 534.7 MiB for muta-duo-q's. Both measured figures are now in
the comment, with the note that C4's mlock_front_mib must use the lock range.

Minors folded in: the [tier] ready trace moves above the publish that wakes
waiters so cause precedes effect; tier_acquire re-checks under the lock before
announcing a wait, so a tier that arrives in the gap no longer prints a
phantom "waiting" line; loader_joiner sets loader_stop before joining, so an
early return skips not-yet-started loads instead of sitting through them; and
the TTFT stamp moves above llama_decode -- that decode is prefill for the NEXT
token and was inflating G11's headline number by a decode step.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5
